configured_status evidence .claude/settings.json read 20260916; tools/f/fleet-loop.sh execs --effort medium on this pier
voice Kyri
host Framework laptop, NixOS, America/New_York
title Erratum -- the body that named the wrong parent, and the day that would not close
prompt Same lap, after the send: the follow-up commit's own body was read back against the card.
rota lap 5192, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees. The second reading of the same row: look at what you just shipped before believing it.
think The close commit ae3fccd5a says its Git nib was rewritten to 71a556d85c and names that as its own parent. The card and the parent both read 397037a129. The hash entered the body BEFORE the send's final rebase; the rebase replayed onto a peer's work and the amend ran --no-edit.
think The commit-message wall reads a body's PATHS against the tree and never its hashes, and an object check would have welcomed this one anyway -- 71a556d85c is a live commit here. Only the relation the sentence asserts is false.
think Booked, and the booking met the derived spine's founding case twice in one send: %800 was taken, a peer had bound it half an hour earlier, so the unshared row renumbered to %801; and the same rebase carried a fold of %798 that the peer had already shelved, so that fold was withdrawn whole rather than landed twice.
think The pin then stood 330 bytes over its 65536. Trimming my own unshared row bought 300 and no more, so %799 folded to a shelf of its own, which is what the ledger's header says the cure is -- close rows rather than carry them.
obs The cold endurance run outlasted the lap, at 150 guards read of 371 when the work landed. Its later readings are of a MOVING tree, since it walks the live checkout while the lap edits it, so it is not a baseline for this round and is not reported as one.
obs Seven of its readings were red. Four were re-run singly on the landed tree: reds_pin_capacity, remember_git_nib and itinerary_list GREEN, log_has_a_row RED for a real reason -- pin_open_day=20260916 against newest_shelf=20260917.
obs The day would not close itself. Opening 20260917 while leaving 20260916 marked open put the pin one day behind the room, and the guard reads exactly that. 20260916 closed at 95 rows against 95 logs, agreed by count in both rosters.
obs Deferring the close was the wrong call and the guard said so within the hour. A day rolls at midnight whatever a lap prefers, and the open marker is a fact about the room rather than a courtesy to peers still writing.
loom guard=cold_endurance_run guards_read=150 of=371 reds=7 verdict=outlasted_the_lap
file construction/REDS.md row %801 booked, headline and span to 801
file construction/archive/REDS-a-printed-line-is-not-a-read-one-rows-799.md %799 folded to hold the pin under 65536
file session-logs/README.md 20260916 closed at 95, 20260917 open
file session-logs/CHAPTERS.md the same close, derived from the shelf's own rows
scope one ledger row, one fold, the day close; no code touched
status GREEN -- reds_ledger_monotone, reds_fold, reds_citation, unshared_citation, link_text_promise, tracked_link, log_has_a_row, session_roster_agree, index_row_bound, index_fold, remember_git_nib, reds_pin_capacity, itinerary_list all GREEN on the landed tree
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- whether a commit body's own hash claims earn a wall is a fleet question, and the body stands as testimony either way
